    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the mechanism of action for ACE inhibitors like Lisinopril? I'm specifically trying to understand how they interact with the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) to reduce blood pressure.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ACE inhibitors like Lisinopril work by blocking the Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE). This enzyme is crucial for converting Angiotensin I into Angiotensin II, a potent vasoconstrictor. By inhibiting this conversion, Lisinopril prevents the formation of Angiotensin II, which in turn leads to vasodilation and a reduction in blood pressure. Furthermore, this action also decreases aldosterone secretion, thereby reducing sodium and water retention.",
      "source": "target",
