    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that makes sense. Could you go a bit deeper into the RAAS pathway? Specifically, how does the decrease in Angiotensin II lead to that reduction in aldosterone secretion you mentioned? I'm a bit fuzzy on the exact connection there.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The RAAS pathway, when it's being overactive, is a cascade that starts with the kidneys releasing renin, which kicks off the whole chain reaction. Renin acts on angiotensinogen to form Angiotensin I, which is then converted by ACE into the main culprit, Angiotensin II. Angiotensin II is a powerful stimulator of the adrenal glands to release aldosterone.\n\nWhen Lisinopril blocks ACE, the production of Angiotensin II plummets, and this lack of the strong signal directly tells the adrenal glands to secrete less aldosterone. Aldosterone's job is to make the kidneys retain sodium and water, which increases blood volume and therefore blood pressure. So, by starving the system of Angiotensin II, Lisinopril effectively dampens the aldosterone signal, leading to less fluid retention and lower blood pressure.",
      "source": "target",
