    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, I think I'm following the chain of events. To make sure I have the full picture for my exam, can you walk me through the step-by-step physiological consequences of that decrease in aldosterone? Like, what exactly is happening at the level of the nephron when those sodium and water levels change?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When aldosterone levels decrease, the effects on the nephron are quite significant, leading to a diuresis, or increased urine output. Normally, high aldosterone acts on the principal cells of the late distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct, prompting them to increase the reabsorption of sodium ($\\text{Na}^+$) and water. The reduction in aldosterone means these cells are less stimulated to hoard salt and water.\n\nConsequently, less sodium is reabsorbed back into the bloodstream, and because water follows sodium osmotically, less water is retained in the body. This increased excretion of sodium and water through the urine reduces the overall plasma volume, which is the direct mechanism for lowering blood pressure. This is a fundamental way these drugs manage hypertension.",
      "source": "target",
